The main difference lies in whether you are using a standard library or talking directly to the Operating System (OS).
main and _start are related, but they are not the same thing.
Think of it like this:
Operating System     
↓   
_start        ← real entry point      
↓
runtime setup (libc / CRT)     
↓    
main         ← your C/C++ function      
↓
runtime cleanup      
↓   
exit()

1. _start = real program entry point

When the OS loads your executable (ELF on Linux, PE on Windows), it does NOT jump to main.

It jumps to a low-level symbol called something like:

_start (Linux ELF)
start
WinMainCRTStartup
mainCRTStartup

depending on platform/compiler/runtime.

This is the actual first instruction executed.

In pure assembly, you often define _start yourself:

global _start

section .text
_start:
    mov rax, 60     ; sys_exit
    mov rdi, 0
    syscall

No C runtime exists here.

You directly talk to the kernel.

2. main = C/C++ runtime entry

main is just a normal function expected by the C/C++ runtime.

Example:

int main() {
    return 0;
}

But somebody must:

prepare stack
initialize libc
initialize iostream
initialize global constructors
parse command line arguments
setup environment variables
call main
call destructors after main
terminate process correctly

That "somebody" is _start + runtime library.

3. Why sometimes you see _start

You usually see _start when:

writing pure assembly
reverse engineering
using -nostdlib
making tiny executables
OS development
malware/reversing challenges
embedded systems

Because there is no C runtime.

Example:

gcc test.s -nostdlib

Now YOU must provide _start.

4. Why sometimes you only see main

Normal C/C++ compilation automatically links runtime startup code.

Example:

g++ main.cpp

The linker secretly adds startup files like:

crt1.o
crti.o
crtn.o

These contain _start.

So your code only needs:

int main()

5. Linux example
Your source
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello\n";
}
Real execution flow
kernel
  ↓
_start          (from crt1.o)
  ↓
__libc_start_main
  ↓
main

__libc_start_main is part of glibc.

It handles runtime initialization.

6. Windows equivalent

Windows usually uses:

mainCRTStartup
    ↓
main

or GUI programs:

WinMainCRTStartup
    ↓
WinMain

The CRT = C Runtime.

7. Important difference
_start	main
Actual entry point	User function
Called by OS loader	Called by runtime
No runtime initialized yet	Runtime already initialized
Works without libc	Depends on libc/CRT
Usually assembly-level	Usually C/C++
Must exit manually	Can return
8. Why returning from _start is dangerous

In main:

return 0;

works because runtime converts it to:

exit(0)

But in _start:

ret

is invalid unless you set up a proper caller.

Because _start was entered directly by the kernel, not by a normal call.

So in _start, you usually do:

mov rax, 60
xor rdi, rdi
syscall
9. Reverse engineering note

In IDA/Ghidra, sometimes you see:

_start
  →
__libc_start_main
  →
main

because disassemblers show the true entry point.

Beginners often wonder:

"Why isn't main first?"

Because main is not the actual entry point of the executable.

_start is.

why is the task with only start and no main is categorize as c/c++ assembly ?
Because C/C++ programs and assembly programs are often mixed together at the binary/runtime level.

A program that only has _start is usually considered:

a pure assembly program
or a freestanding/no-runtime C program

not a normal hosted C++ program.

But tools, tutorials, or reverse engineers may still loosely call it “C/C++ assembly” because it uses:

the same executable format (ELF/PE)
the same ABI/calling convention
the same linker
the same startup model used by C/C++
The important distinction

There are actually 3 layers here:

Layer	Example
Language	C++, C, Assembly
Runtime	libc / CRT
Binary interface	ELF, PE, ABI

main belongs mostly to the runtime/language convention.

_start belongs to the OS + ABI convention.

Normal C/C++ program

Example:

int main() {
    return 0;
}

Compiler adds runtime startup:

_start → libc startup → main

This is called a hosted environment.

You rely on:

libc
CRT
runtime initialization
Pure assembly program

Example:

global _start

section .text
_start:
    mov rax, 60
    xor rdi, rdi
    syscall

No runtime.

No libc.

No main.

This is just:

kernel
executable loader
your code
But C can also work without main

You can write C with _start directly:

void _start() {
    __asm__(
        "mov $60, %rax\n"
        "xor %rdi, %rdi\n"
        "syscall"
    );
}

compiled with:

gcc test.c -nostdlib

Now this is:

written in C
but behaves like raw assembly/runtime-free code

This is called:

freestanding C
bare-metal style
no CRT
Why people group them together

Because at low level:

C compilers generate assembly
Assembly follows the same ABI
Both eventually become machine code
Both use _start
Both interact with linker/loader similarly

Especially in:

reverse engineering
OS development
exploit development
compiler internals

people blur the line between:

“assembly”
“low-level C”
“runtime startup code”
Key idea: main is NOT required by the OS

The OS only needs:

an executable
a valid entry point address

In ELF:

e_entry

points to _start.

The kernel does not know or care about main.

main is purely a convention created by the C runtime.

So why “only _start” feels like assembly

Because once you remove the runtime:

you manually handle syscalls
manually exit
manually manage stack/registers
manually initialize things

which is traditionally assembly territory.

Even if written in C.

Analogy

Think of:

Situation	Analogy
main	Driving a car normally
_start	Building the engine and ignition yourself

With main, the runtime already prepared everything.

With _start, you're directly interfacing with the machine/OS.






